   desc="The 1832 volume (dated 1833) containing 'The Lady of Shalott', 'The Palace of Art', 'Oenone', 'The Lotos-Eaters' and 'A Dream of Fair Women' — the volume attacked by John Wilson Croker in the Quarterly Review in 1833, and the one whose ten years of revision produced the 1842 Poems.",
   plot="The poems are symbolic narratives: the Lady of Shalott weaves a mirror-image of the world until the curse falls on her; the Palace of Art is a soul's self-built retreat which decays; the Lotos-Eaters turn Homeric episode into a moral argument for withdrawal and oblivion; Oenone speaks the wrongs of Helen from the mountain.",
